  "mechanismSummary": "In the Hantzsch synthesis two molecules of ethyl acetoacetate, an aldehyde (here formaldehyde) and ammonia build a pyridine. One ketoester condenses with the aldehyde (a Knoevenagel reaction) and the other with ammonia (an enamine); those two steps are not drawn here. The enamine adds to the Knoevenagel product in a Michael addition; proton transfers give back the ketone and the enamine; the NH₂ group attacks the ketone to close a six-membered ring; and loss of water and a proton gives the 1,4-dihydropyridine (the Hantzsch ester). Oxidation (not drawn) then gives the aromatic pyridine.",
  "references": [
    "Clayden, Greeves and Warren, Organic Chemistry, 2nd edition (Oxford University Press), chapter 30"
  ],
